The '''Welsh Romani language''', or {{lang|rmw|'''Romnimus'''}}, is a dialect of the [[Romani language]] spoken by the [[Kale (Welsh Gypsies)|Kale]] subgroup of the [[Romani people]], who have been present in [[Wales]] since the 16th century. Welsh Romani is one of the many [[Northern Romani dialects]].<ref>Norbert Boretzky: Kommentierter Dialektatlas des Romani. Wiesbaden: Harrassowitz, 2004 p. 18</ref>

While most of its vocabulary is of [[Indo-Aryan languages|Indo-Aryan]] origin, there are a significant number of words derived from other languages, such as [[Persian language|Persian]] and [[Greek language|Greek]]. [[Welsh language|Welsh]] loanwords include ''melanō'' ("yellow", from ''melyn''), ''grīga'' ("heather", from ''grug'') and ''kraŋka'' ("crab", from ''cranc''). [[English language|English]] loanwords include ''vlija'' ("village"), ''spīdra'' ("spider") and ''bråmla'' ("bramble").<ref>[[John Sampson (linguist)|John Sampson]] (1926) ''The dialect of the Gypsies of Wales, being the older form of British Romani preserved in the speech of the clan of Abram Wood'', Oxford University Press, London.</ref>

Historically the variants of Welsh Romani and [[Anglo-Romani|Angloromani]] (spoken by the [[Romanichal]]) constituted the same variant of Romani, known as British Romani.<ref>Sampson. J. (1926) The Dialect of the Gypsies of Wales. Oxford. Clarendon Press.</ref> Welsh Romani is closely related to [[Angloromani language|Angloromani]], [[Scandoromani]] (spoken by [[Romanisael]] in Sweden and Norway), [[Scottish Cant]] (spoken by [[Scottish Romani and Traveller groups|Scottish Lowland Romani]] in Lowland Scotland) and [[Finnish Romani language|Kalo]] (Spoken by [[Kaale (Finnish Romani)|Kaale]] in Finland and Sweden). Kale, Romanichal, Romanisael, Kaale and Scottish Lowland Romani are closely related Romani subgroups which stem from the wave of Romani immigrants who arrived in [[Northern Europe]] in the 16th century.<ref>Bakker (1997) Review of McGowan, The Winchester Confessions. Journal of the Gypsy Lore Society. Fifth series, 7. (1): 49–50.</ref>

